Excess-vocabulary data
----------------------

`techlint/data/ai_excess_vocab.json` is derived from the dataset published
with:

    Kobak D., González-Márquez R., Horvát E.-Á., Lause J. (2025).
    "Delving into LLM-assisted writing in biomedical publications through
    excess vocabulary." Science Advances 11(30).

Source data: https://github.com/berenslab/llm-excess-vocab — MIT License.

techlint reproduces the paper's counterfactual (a linear extrapolation of the
2021→2022 frequency trend into 2024) to compute a per-word excess ratio, then
tiers the words by effect size. The regeneration script is
`tools/build_excess_vocab.py`.

Calibration corpus
------------------

`benchmarks/fetch.py` downloads IETF RFCs and Python PEPs at build time. These
texts are not redistributed here — `benchmarks/corpus/` is gitignored. RFCs are
published under the IETF Trust Legal Provisions; PEPs are placed in the public
domain or licensed under CC0 by their authors.

ASD-STE100
----------

Earlier versions of this project implemented ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical
English) directly. That layer was removed; see `docs/removed-rules.md`.

ASD-STE100 is copyright ASD (AeroSpace, Security and Defence Industries
Association of Europe) and is free to obtain from https://www.asd-ste100.org.
No text or data from that specification is distributed in this repository. The
handful of surviving rules that ASD-STE100 also happens to recommend are
included because independent authorities (Google, Microsoft, the Federal Plain
Language Guidelines, RFC 2119, Gopen & Swan) recommend them as well; each is
cited in `docs/research-basis.md`.

Ideas
-----

The severity model, weighted score, suppression baseline, exemption taxonomy,
and calibration discipline are adapted from two sibling projects that apply the
same code-smell framing to fiction.

Reference validation, the axis breakdown, and the scaffolding-ratio check are
adapted from sloppylint (https://github.com/rsionnach/sloppylint), MIT
licensed, which performs the equivalent job for Python code.

No code from any of these projects is included; the debt is conceptual.
